#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""nafeel-theme: the shell's theme engine.

One token document, every surface. The palette step (wallpaper-palette's
extract.py + pigment.py) gives the Material scheme and the wallpaper's
pigments; this turns them, with the shell's settings, into theme.json (the
shell reads it) and renders the app templates from the same tokens.

Built on all three engines it replaces or learned from:
  Noctalia   the template language (matugen-compatible: colors.<role>.<mode>.
             <format>, filters), its template manifests (template.toml:
             input/output paths, requires_path, post hooks, the KDE colour
             scheme merge) and its built-in templates (MIT, theme/templates/
             builtin), so its community templates work unchanged
  Ryoku      core templates always render; app templates sit behind one
             switch ("apps"); kdeglobals is merged, never replaced
  Caelestia  open terminals are recoloured live (OSC sequences to every pty),
             `{{ $role }}` templates, a user template dir, one post hook

and the shell's own tokens on top, so a template can follow the shell rather
than only Material roles:
  colors.paper_0..4 / paper   the sheets (surface container levels / surface)
  colors.ink / ink_sub / ink_faint / rule      text and hairlines
  colors.lead / pop / second  the wallpaper's real pigments (accent roles
                              when the wallpaper has none)
  {{ alpha.base }} {{ alpha.layers }}         the frosted paper's opacity
  {{ alpha.apps }} {{ alpha.gtk }}            apps' window opacity (the Theme
                                              page; gtk is 1 unless GTK apps
                                              are made translucent too)
  {{ radius.control|card|panel|frame }}       the corner radius setting
  {{ font.sans|serif|mono|cjk }}              the type
  short forms: {{ paper.2.hex }}, {{ ink.sub.rgba }}, {{ accent.lead.hex }}
  a colour filter takes a token as its number: | set_alpha alpha.base

Usage:
  nafeel-theme update  [--wallpaper PATH] [--force]   the whole step: palette
                       (only when the wallpaper or variant changed), build, apply
  nafeel-theme update --stage --wallpaper PATH   make theme-next.json only (the
                       shell stages a wallpaper while its picker is open)
  nafeel-theme commit  theme-next.json becomes theme.json; apply
  nafeel-theme build   [--scheme FILE] [--mode dark|light]   write theme.json
  nafeel-theme apply   [--force] [--only ID ...] [--dry-run DIR]
  nafeel-theme render  TEMPLATE [--mode dark|light]           print one
  nafeel-theme list   [--json]                               what's known
  nafeel-theme catalog                     the community catalog (JSON, cached a day)

Files:
  ~/.local/state/nafeel-shell/theme.json        the token document
  ~/.config/nafeel-shell/theme.toml             which templates, live options
  ~/.config/nafeel-shell/templates/             your own (Caelestia-style):
                                                 every file is rendered to
                                                 ~/.local/state/nafeel-shell/
                                                 themes/<name>
  ~/.local/share/nafeel-shell/templates/community/<id>   community templates
"""

